    "American Medical Association. Code of Medical Ethics Opinion 8.3, Physicians' Responsibilities in Disaster Response & Preparedness. Individual physicians have an obligation to provide urgent medical care during disasters, holding even against greater than usual risk to their own safety, health or life; they also have an obligation to weigh the risk of providing care in the present against the need to remain available to provide care in the future.": {"url":"https://code-medical-ethics.ama-assn.org/ethics-opinions/physicians-responsibilities-disaster-response-preparedness","verified_by":"official-source-page","checked_at":"2026-08-30"},
    "American Medical Association. Policy H-480.931, Assessing the Intersection Between AI and Health Care, current through 2026. Transparency and disclosure decisions \"should be based upon a risk- and impact-based approach,\" with the need for disclosure greater where an AI-enabled technology carries greater risk of harm to a patient. Note that much of what H-480.931 enumerates is disclosure by the AI developer to purchasers and physicians; the risk-and-impact principle is what this candidate carries across to physician-to-patient disclosure.": {"url":"https://policysearch.ama-assn.org/policyfinder/detail/augmented%20intelligence?uri=%2FAMADoc%2FHOD.xml-H-480.931.xml","verified_by":"official-source-page","checked_at":"2026-08-30"},
